.he "mv (1)                       8/16/26                       mv (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
mv
- move or rename files
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
mv [ -i ] [ -f ] [ - ] file1 file2
.bd
mv [ -i ] [ -f ] [ - ] file ... directory
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Mv moves (changes the name of) file1 to file2.  If file2 already
exists, it is removed first.  If file2 has a mode that forbids
writing, mv prints the mode and asks; a line beginning with y lets the
move go on, and anything else stops it.  Mv refuses to move a file
onto itself.
.sp
In the second form, one or more files are moved to directory under
their own names.
.sp
If the two names are on different file systems, mv must copy the file
and delete the original.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-i	Ask before replacing an existing file; only a y answer lets it happen.

.ti -10
-f	Force: override -i and the check of a file's write permission.

.ti -10
-	Take every following argument as a file name, so that a name beginning with a minus sign can be moved.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
A directory can be renamed only within its own parent.  Rename here is
unlink and link, and a directory's .. entry would otherwise point at
the wrong parent.
.sp
Across file systems the copied file belongs to whoever runs mv, its
links to other files are lost, and its modification time is now -
micronix has no call to set a time.
.sp
The prompts appear only when the input is a terminal.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
cp (1), ln (1)
